From: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Subject: [PATCH] hwcomposer window: treat setBufferCount(0) as "use the default"

Mali's libGLES_mali.so on the Spreadtrum SC7730S (goyavewifi) calls
native_window_set_buffer_count(window, 0) while it creates the window surface.
In Android's BufferQueue a count of 0 means "go back to the default count".
HWComposerNativeWindow took it literally: it destroyed its buffers, set
m_bufferCount to 0, and the next dequeueBuffer() allocated no buffers at all,
so `assert(!m_bufList.empty())` aborted the compositor (surface-manager).

Keep the default of two buffers when the caller asks for none.

Signed-off-by: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Upstream-Status: Pending
---
 egl/platforms/hwcomposer/hwcomposer_window.cpp | 4 ++++
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+)

--- a/egl/platforms/hwcomposer/hwcomposer_window.cpp
+++ b/egl/platforms/hwcomposer/hwcomposer_window.cpp
@@ -473,6 +473,10 @@
  */
 int HWComposerNativeWindow::setBufferCount(int count)
 {
+    // 0 means "default count" in BufferQueue, not "no buffers".
+    if (count <= 0)
+        count = 2;
+
     int need_realloc = (unsigned int) count != m_bufferCount;
     TRACE("count=%d realloc=%d", count, need_realloc);
     m_bufferCount = count;
